      "heading": "Sensitivity depends on what must be preserved",
      "body": "Bede's Kraken2 benchmarks show how strongly host-removal performance depends on the database and its parameters. Large databases with dense minimiser sampling can classify human reads very sensitively, but can also remove bacterial and viral reads. Shared sequences introduce another complication: reads matching multiple genomes may receive a taxonomic assignment that does not identify them as human. A human-only database addresses that ambiguity, but not excessive classification of microbial reads as human.\n\nThe acceptable trade-off depends on the biological question. Bede uses Mycobacterium tuberculosis as an example with little sequence overlap with humans, allowing aggressive filtering at relatively little cost. Broad metagenomics is harder: viruses may share sequence with endogenous retroviral elements in the human genome. A filter suitable for one bacterium may therefore be destructive when looking for an unknown range of pathogens."
